Adaptive Capsule-Based Artificial Intelligence: Brain-Inspired Plasticity, Self-Generation, and Emergent Distributed Cognition — A Mathematical Framework

Ouadi Maakoul

---

Abstract

This thesis develops a rigorous mathematical framework for a self-generating neural architecture inspired by biological plasticity. The architecture consists of vector capsules interconnected by adaptive highways, governed by local plasticity rules and a distributed consensus mechanism (the Nucleus) that generates new capsules when functional gaps are detected. A capacity-utilisation brake provably bounds growth in non-stationary environments via a direct summability argument.

The system is formalised as a port-Hamiltonian dynamical system with a symmetric, strictly positive definite damping matrix \mathbf{R} = \kappa\mathbf{I} + \mathbf{A}\mathbf{A}^\top that guarantees passivity and asymptotic stability. Stability under structural adaptation is handled through a hybrid LaSalle framework with an explicit minimum dwell-time condition. The finite-event property follows from the capacity brake, not from a decaying insertion rate.

A central theoretical contribution is the Blind Spot Theorem: any bounded cognitive system with hypothesis space \mathcal{H} \subsetneq W necessarily has a blind spot set of positive measure. We prove algorithmic undetectability via a formal \sigma-algebra separation argument. A constructive meta-expansion operator, driven by anomaly-based density sampling, compresses this blind spot monotonically with high probability, provided the observation mapping satisfies an absolute-continuity condition. An information-theoretic lower bound on the irreducible residual is given using a rate-distortion covering-number argument.

Emergent distributed cognition is quantified using Continuous Causal Emergence 2.0, restricted to a low-dimensional projected state space to ensure that k-NN entropy estimators converge at tractable rates. A signed hierarchical apportionment — in which upward and downward causal contributions are proved to be non-negative and sum to one — leads to a normalised distributed intelligence metric \tilde{\mathcal{D}} \in [0,2].

Routing convergence is proved to yield a unique fixed point under a strict concavity condition, and the two-timescale analysis is extended to Markovian activation noise. All proofs are self-contained. No empirical experiments are performed; the contribution is purely foundational.

