Chapter 2: Mathematical Preliminaries and Related Frameworks

2.1 Neural Plasticity as Dynamical Systems

Let a neuron be characterised by an activity variable x \in \mathbb{R}. A synapse from pre-synaptic neuron i to post-synaptic neuron j has weight w_{ij} \in \mathbb{R}.

Definition 2.1 (Hebbian Rule).

\frac{dw_{ij}}{dt} = \eta x_i x_j - \gamma w_{ij}, \quad \eta > 0,\; \gamma \ge 0.

Definition 2.2 (Covariance Rule).

\frac{dw_{ij}}{dt} = \eta(x_i - \langle x_i\rangle)(x_j - \langle x_j\rangle),

where \langle\cdot\rangle is a running average.

Definition 2.3 (BCM Rule).

\frac{dw_{ij}}{dt} = \eta x_i x_j(x_j - \theta_j), \quad \frac{d\theta_j}{dt} = \frac{1}{\tau}(x_j^2 - \theta_j).

The sliding threshold \theta_j provides homeostatic stability; the BCM dynamics admit a Lyapunov function \mathcal{L} = \sum_j \frac{1}{2}\theta_j^2 whose time derivative is \dot{\mathcal{L}} = \frac{1}{\tau}\sum_j \theta_j(x_j^2 - \theta_j), which is sign-definite under the BCM fixed-point condition \theta_j = \langle x_j^2\rangle.

Definition 2.4 (STDP — Trace Model).
Trace dynamics: \frac{d\,\text{tr}}{dt} = -\frac{\text{tr}}{\tau} + x(t). Weight update:

\Delta w_{ij} = A_+\,\text{tr}_{\text{pre}}\,x_{\text{post}} - A_-\,\text{tr}_{\text{post}}\,x_{\text{pre}}.

These rules are extended to matrix-valued weights in Chapter 4.

2.2 Structural Plasticity

SOINN (Self-Organising Incremental Neural Networks) inserts nodes when local accumulated error exceeds a threshold. GWR (Grow-When-Required) inserts a neuron when best-matching-unit activation falls below a threshold. Both lack vector capsules, dynamic routing, and measure-theoretic epistemic gap detection. NEAT evolves topologies offline via genetic algorithms. Predictive coding / active inference (Rao & Ballard 1999; Friston 2005) minimises variational free energy, with high surprise signalling the need for model expansion; our Blind Spot Theorem provides a measure-theoretic foundation for this intuition.

2.3 Capsule Networks and Dynamic Routing

Definition 2.5 (Capsule, Sabour et al. 2017). A capsule i outputs \mathbf{u}_i = \text{squash}(\mathbf{s}_i), where \mathbf{s}_i = \mathbf{W}_i\mathbf{x} and

\text{squash}(\mathbf{s}) = \frac{\|\mathbf{s}\|^2}{1+\|\mathbf{s}\|^2}\cdot\frac{\mathbf{s}}{\|\mathbf{s}\|}.

For analytical tractability we replace squash with the smooth surrogate \tilde{f} (Definition 4.1).

Dynamic Routing. For lower capsule i and higher capsule j, prediction vector \hat{\mathbf{u}}_{j|i} = \mathbf{W}_{ij}\mathbf{u}_i. Routing iterates: c_{ij} = \text{softmax}_j(b_{ij}), \mathbf{s}_j = \sum_i c_{ij}\hat{\mathbf{u}}_{j|i}, \mathbf{v}_j = \tilde{f}(\mathbf{s}_j), b_{ij} \leftarrow b_{ij} + \mathbf{v}_j\cdot\hat{\mathbf{u}}_{j|i}.

Remark on the routing objective. The routing algorithm maximises a concave objective \mathcal{L}(\mathbf{c}) = \sum_j \mathbf{v}_j \cdot \mathbf{s}_j subject to the simplex constraint \sum_j c_{ij} = 1 for each i. Since the objective is strictly concave under the conditions stated in Theorem 4.1, it has a unique global maximum, and the routing algorithm converges to this unique point. This is established rigorously in Theorem 4.1 below. Earlier analyses (Ye et al. 2025, arXiv 2501.06240) confirm the concavity structure and show that without strict concavity the algorithm may merely polarise link strengths; we close this gap by verifying strict concavity under our barrier potential.

2.4 Port-Hamiltonian Systems

Definition 2.6 (Port-Hamiltonian System). Let \mathbf{x} \in \mathbb{R}^n, H(\mathbf{x}) the Hamiltonian. Dynamics:

\dot{\mathbf{x}} = (\mathbf{J}(\mathbf{x}) - \mathbf{R}(\mathbf{x}))\nabla H(\mathbf{x}) + \mathbf{g}(\mathbf{x})\mathbf{u},

with \mathbf{J}^\top = -\mathbf{J} (skew-symmetric interconnection) and \mathbf{R} \succeq 0 (positive semidefinite damping).

Theorem 2.1 (Passivity). With output \mathbf{y} = \mathbf{g}^\top\nabla H, we have \dot{H} \le \mathbf{u}^\top\mathbf{y}. For \mathbf{u}=0, H is non-increasing.

Our contribution uses \mathbf{R} = \kappa\mathbf{I} + \mathbf{A}\mathbf{A}^\top (symmetric positive definite for all \kappa > 0) and applies the hybrid LaSalle framework for topology-switching systems to handle structural adaptation.

2.5 Causal Emergence and Its Extensions

Hoel et al. (2013) introduced causal emergence for discrete Markov chains. Hoel et al. (2024) extended effective information and causal power to continuous dynamics. Our Continuous Causal Emergence 2.0 further adds a signed hierarchical apportionment with non-negativity guarantees and a normalised metric estimated in a projected low-dimensional space to avoid the curse of dimensionality.

2.6 Hybrid Dynamical Systems and LaSalle

Definition 2.7 (Hybrid System with Dwell-Time). A hybrid dynamical system is a tuple (\mathcal{C}, F, \mathcal{D}, G) where \mathcal{C} is the flow set, F the flow map, \mathcal{D} the jump set, and G the jump map. A solution has minimum dwell-time \tau_d > 0 if between any two consecutive jumps at times t_k and t_{k+1}, we have t_{k+1} - t_k \ge \tau_d.

Theorem 2.2 (Hybrid LaSalle, Goebel-Sanfelice-Teel 2012). Let V: \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} be a Lyapunov function satisfying (i) \dot{V} \le -\alpha V during flows for some \alpha > 0, (ii) V(G(\mathbf{x})) \le V(\mathbf{x}) + \Delta V_{\max} at each jump, (iii) there are at most finitely many jumps. Then every bounded solution converges to the largest invariant set of \{V = \text{const}\}.

This framework is the correct tool for Chapter 5, replacing the direct application of classical LaSalle to a topology-changing system.

