Chapter 3: Epistemic Foundations — The Blind Spot Theorem and Constructive Meta-Expansion

3.1 Formal Setting

Let (W, \mathcal{F}, \mu) be a probability space. The agent's hypothesis space \mathcal{H} \in \mathcal{F} satisfies \mu(\mathcal{H}) < 1. The agent's dynamics are given by a Markov kernel T: W \to \Delta(W) with T(\mathcal{H}) \subseteq \mathcal{H}. Given s_0 \in \mathcal{H}, let \mathcal{R}_n = T^n(\delta_{s_0}) and \mathcal{R}(s_0) = \bigcup_{n\ge 0}\operatorname{supp}(\mathcal{R}_n). Let \overline{\mathcal{R}(s_0)} denote the smallest \mathcal{F}-closed set whose complement has \mu-measure zero.

Definition 3.1 (Blind Spot Set).

\mathcal{B} := W \setminus \overline{\mathcal{R}(s_0)}.

3.2 Necessity of Blind Spots

Theorem 3.1 (Blind Spot Necessity). If \mu(\mathcal{H}) < 1, then \mu(\mathcal{B}) > 0.

Proof. Since T(\mathcal{H}) \subseteq \mathcal{H}, for any n \ge 0, \operatorname{supp}(\mathcal{R}_n) \subseteq \mathcal{H}. Therefore \bigcup_{n\ge 0}\operatorname{supp}(\mathcal{R}_n) \subseteq \mathcal{H}, hence \overline{\mathcal{R}(s_0)} \subseteq \overline{\mathcal{H}}. Since \mu(\overline{\mathcal{H}}) = \mu(\mathcal{H}) < 1 (taking \mathcal{H} to be \mathcal{F}-measurable), we get \mu(\overline{\mathcal{R}(s_0)}) \le \mu(\mathcal{H}) < 1, so \mu(\mathcal{B}) = 1 - \mu(\overline{\mathcal{R}(s_0)}) > 0. \square

3.3 Algorithmic Undetectability

We now give a formal proof that replaces the circular measurability argument of earlier drafts.

Definition 3.2 (Internal Algorithm). Let \sigma(\mathcal{H}) denote the sub-\sigma-algebra of \mathcal{F} generated by \mathcal{H}:

\sigma(\mathcal{H}) := \{B \cap \mathcal{H} : B \in \mathcal{F}\} \cup \{\emptyset, \mathcal{H}, W \setminus \mathcal{H}, W\}.

An internal algorithm is a Turing machine M that receives oracle access to s_0, the restriction T|_{\mathcal{H}} (i.e., it can query T(h, \cdot) for any h \in \mathcal{H}), and finitely many samples drawn from measures that are absolutely continuous with respect to \mu|_{\mathcal{H}}. Hence every measure that M can access has support contained in \mathcal{H}.

Theorem 3.2 (Algorithmic Undetectability). For any internal algorithm M, the set of w \in \mathcal{B} that M correctly identifies as belonging to \mathcal{B} with probability greater than \frac{1}{2} has \mu-measure zero.

Proof. We argue by \sigma-algebra separation.

Step 1: Any function f: W \to \{0,1\} computed by M after finitely many oracle queries is \sigma(\mathcal{H})-measurable. This follows because each oracle query returns values in \mathcal{H}, so the information available to M is entirely contained in \sigma(\mathcal{H}). A finite composition of \sigma(\mathcal{H})-measurable operations remains \sigma(\mathcal{H})-measurable.

Step 2: We claim \mathbf{1}_{\mathcal{B}} is not \sigma(\mathcal{H})-measurable. The atoms of \sigma(\mathcal{H}) are: \mathcal{H} itself, and the singletons \{w\} for w \in W \setminus \mathcal{H} (more precisely, the equivalence classes of points in W \setminus \mathcal{H} that are indistinguishable by \sigma(\mathcal{H})-measurable functions). Because the agent has access only to information from \mathcal{H}, any \sigma(\mathcal{H})-measurable function cannot distinguish between different points in W \setminus \mathcal{H}; thus the only \sigma(\mathcal{H})-measurable subsets of W \setminus \mathcal{H} are \emptyset and the whole set W \setminus \mathcal{H}. Since \mathcal{B} \subseteq W \setminus \mathcal{H} and \mathcal{B} has positive \mu-measure while being a strict subset in general, \mathcal{B} cannot be \sigma(\mathcal{H})-measurable.

Step 3: Suppose for contradiction that M outputs \hat{\chi} which equals \mathbf{1}_{\mathcal{B}} on a set S \subseteq \mathcal{B} with \mu(S) > 0. Then \hat{\chi}^{-1}(\{1\}) \cap \mathcal{B} = S is a \sigma(\mathcal{H})-measurable set of positive measure lying entirely in W \setminus \mathcal{H}. This contradicts the fact that the only \sigma(\mathcal{H})-measurable subsets of W \setminus \mathcal{H} are of measure zero or full measure in W \setminus \mathcal{H}. Hence no such M can succeed on a positive-measure subset of \mathcal{B}. \square

Remark 3.1. This proof does not invoke Gödel incompleteness or Rice's theorem; it relies solely on the information-theoretic partition induced by the \sigma-algebra.

3.4 Anomaly Functions as Observable Signatures

Definition 3.3 (Separating Anomaly Function). A function A: W \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} is separating if

\inf_{w \in \mathcal{B}} A(w) > \sup_{h \in \mathcal{H}} A(h).

A canonical example: A(w) = -\log p_\mathcal{H}(O(w)), the negative log-likelihood under the agent's generative model, where O: W \to \mathcal{X} is the observation mapping.

Proposition 3.1. If A is separating and \theta = \sup_{h \in \mathcal{H}} A(h), then the event \{A(w) > \theta\} is a necessary condition for w \in \mathcal{B}. Persistent high-anomaly events in a region of \mathcal{X} provide probabilistic evidence of a blind spot.

3.5 Constructive Meta-Expansion via Density Sampling

Maintain an anomaly buffer \mathcal{D} = \{\mathbf{x}_k\}_{k=1}^K of observed feature vectors triggering A > \tau, where \mathbf{x}_k = O(w_k) for some w_k \in \mathcal{B}.

Assumption 3.1 (Observation Mapping Absolute Continuity). The observation mapping O: W \to \mathcal{X} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n is measurable, and the pushforward measure O_*\mu|_\mathcal{B} is absolutely continuous with respect to the Lebesgue measure on \mathcal{X}. This ensures that modes detected in feature space correspond to genuine regions of positive world-measure.

Definition 3.4 (Meta-Expansion Operator \mathcal{M}).

1. Estimate kernel density \hat{p}(\mathbf{x}) = \frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^K k_h(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{x}_k) on \mathcal{X}.
2. Identify local modes \{\mathbf{m}_\ell\}_{\ell=1}^M of \hat{p} exceeding density threshold \rho.
3. For each mode \mathbf{m}_\ell, create a new capsule with initial weight matrix \mathbf{W}_\text{new} = \mathbf{m}_\ell \mathbf{v}^\top + \delta\mathbf{E}, where \mathbf{v} is a random input direction and \mathbf{E} small noise.
4. Apply gene-regulated connectivity (Section 4.5) to link the new capsule to existing ones.
5. The updated hypothesis space \mathcal{H}' = \mathcal{H} \cup \mathcal{H}_\text{new} where \mathcal{H}_\text{new} is the representational capacity of the added capsules.

Theorem 3.3 (Probabilistic Blind-Spot Compression). Assume (i) A separating; (ii) \mathcal{B} compact with Lipschitz boundary; (iii) \mu|_\mathcal{B} equivalent to Lebesgue measure on \mathcal{B}; (iv) Assumption 3.1 holds; (v) k_h is a smooth bounded-support kernel with bandwidth h chosen to undersmooth. Then for any \delta > 0 there exists K_0 such that for K \ge K_0, with probability at least 1-\delta, the meta-expansion adds capsules whose representational capacity covers a subset of \mathcal{B} of measure at least \epsilon(K) > 0, and \epsilon(K) \to \mu(\mathcal{B}) as K \to \infty.

Proof. By Assumption 3.1, the anomaly buffer \mathcal{D} samples approximately from O_*\mu|_\mathcal{B}, which has a Lebesgue density on \mathcal{X}. The kernel density estimator \hat{p} converges uniformly to this density as K \to \infty (Devroye & Lugosi 2001, Ch. 3). By the mode consistency theorem (Chacón 2015), the estimated modes converge to the true modes of O_*\mu|_\mathcal{B} in Hausdorff distance. Each mode corresponds, by Assumption 3.1, to a region of positive \mu-measure in \mathcal{B}. Each new capsule's receptive field covers a neighbourhood of a mode in \mathcal{X}, which pulls back to a region of positive \mu-measure in \mathcal{B}. Talagrand concentration inequalities give finite-sample control on the mode location error, yielding the stated 1-\delta probability bound. As K \to \infty, all modes are found and \epsilon(K) \to \mu(\mathcal{B}). \square

3.6 Information-Theoretic Lower Bound on the Residual

Theorem 3.4 (Information-Theoretic Residual Bound). Let the agent have C capsules, each with a weight matrix drawn from a compact set \mathcal{W} \subset \mathbb{R}^{d \times n} of metric entropy (rate-distortion dimension) b bits per capsule at distortion level \varepsilon_0; that is, \log \mathcal{N}(\mathcal{W}, \varepsilon_0) \le b. If the covering number of the world state space satisfies \mathcal{N}(W, \varepsilon_0) \ge 2^{Cb+1}, then the irreducible residual blind spot satisfies \mu(\mathcal{B}_\text{res}) \ge \varepsilon_0 > 0.

Proof. The agent's hypothesis space \mathcal{H} is parameterised by the C weight matrices and can distinguish at most 2^{Cb} distinct world states at resolution \varepsilon_0. If \mathcal{N}(W, \varepsilon_0) \ge 2^{Cb+1}, there are at least 2^{Cb+1} disjoint \varepsilon_0-balls in W; the agent can cover at most 2^{Cb} of these. Hence at least one \varepsilon_0-ball, say B_0, is entirely outside \mathcal{H}, and \mu(B_0) \ge \varepsilon_0 (since \mu is equivalent to Lebesgue on W by assumption). Thus \mu(\mathcal{B}_\text{res}) \ge \mu(B_0) \ge \varepsilon_0. \square

