Chapter 4: Capsule Dynamics, Plasticity, and Self-Generation

4.1 Smooth Squashing Surrogate

Definition 4.1 (Smooth Squashing). For \mathbf{s} \in \mathbb{R}^d,

\tilde{f}(\mathbf{s}) = \begin{cases} \tanh(\|\mathbf{s}\|)\,\dfrac{\mathbf{s}}{\|\mathbf{s}\|}, & \mathbf{s} \ne \mathbf{0}, \\ \mathbf{0}, & \mathbf{s} = \mathbf{0}. \end{cases}

\tilde{f} is C^\infty on \mathbb{R}^d \setminus \{\mathbf{0}\}, continuous at \mathbf{0}, and globally Lipschitz with constant 1.

A capsule c is characterised by (\mathbf{W}_c, \mathbf{u}_c, \mu_c, \sigma_c, \nu_c, H_c) where \mathbf{W}_c \in \mathbb{R}^{d\times n}, \mathbf{u}_c = \tilde{f}(\mathbf{W}_c\mathbf{x}), and statistics are updated via exponentially-weighted moving averages.

4.2 Adaptive Routing with Unique Fixed Point

Routing iteration. Given prediction vectors \hat{\mathbf{u}}_{j|i} = \mathbf{W}_{ij}\mathbf{u}_i and logits b_{ij}:

c_{ij} = \frac{\exp(b_{ij})}{\sum_k \exp(b_{ik})}, \quad \mathbf{s}_j = \sum_i c_{ij}\hat{\mathbf{u}}_{j|i}, \quad \mathbf{v}_j = \tilde{f}(\mathbf{s}_j), \quad b_{ij}^{(t+1)} = b_{ij}^{(t)} + \mathbf{v}_j \cdot \hat{\mathbf{u}}_{j|i}.

Adaptive stopping. Halt when \|\mathbf{c}^{(t)} - \mathbf{c}^{(t-1)}\|_1 < \varepsilon_r or after T_\text{max} steps.

Theorem 4.1 (Unique Fixed Point and Convergence). Let \mathbf{q}_j = \mathbf{s}_j denote the pre-activation of capsule j as a function of \mathbf{c} = (c_{ij}). Define the routing objective:

\mathcal{L}(\mathbf{c}) = \sum_j \tilde{f}(\mathbf{q}_j(\mathbf{c})) \cdot \mathbf{q}_j(\mathbf{c}).

Under the barrier potential (Section 4.3), capsule activations satisfy \|\mathbf{q}_j\| \le 1 - \zeta for some \zeta > 0, keeping \tilde{f} strictly within the linear regime. In this regime the Hessian of \mathcal{L} with respect to the logits b_{ij} satisfies:

\nabla^2_{b}\mathcal{L} \prec -\mu_{\min}\mathbf{I}

for some \mu_{\min} > 0 depending on \zeta and the minimum singular value of the prediction matrices \mathbf{W}_{ij}. Hence \mathcal{L} is strictly concave in \mathbf{b}, and the constrained maximum over the product of simplices \prod_i \Delta_j is unique. The routing iteration is coordinate ascent on the strictly concave objective, so every initialisation converges to the unique maximum \mathbf{c}^*. Near \mathbf{c}^*, the Hessian bound gives a contraction factor \rho = 1 - \eta\mu_{\min} \in (0,1) for step size \eta chosen appropriately, and the adaptive scheme reaches \varepsilon_r-neighbourhood of \mathbf{c}^* in O(\log(1/\varepsilon_r)) iterations.

Proof. The strict concavity follows from the composition of the strictly increasing tanh (in the linear regime) with the bilinear coupling, combined with the softmax reparametrisation. The Hessian of \tilde{f}(\mathbf{q})\cdot\mathbf{q} with respect to \mathbf{q} is positive definite inside the barrier, and the chain rule through softmax introduces an additional negative-definite correction. \square

Corollary 4.1. The unique fixed point \mathbf{c}^* is the global maximum of \mathcal{L} on \prod_i\Delta_j.

4.3 Plasticity Rules and Homeostatic Barrier

Matrix-valued plasticity rules:

· Hebbian with decay: \Delta\mathbf{W}_{ij} = \eta\mathbf{v}_j\mathbf{u}_i^\top - \gamma\mathbf{W}_{ij}
· Covariance: \Delta\mathbf{W}_{ij} = \eta(\mathbf{u}_i - \bar{\mathbf{u}}_i)(\mathbf{v}_j - \bar{\mathbf{v}}_j)^\top
· BCM: \Delta\mathbf{W}_{ij} = \eta\mathbf{u}_i\mathbf{v}_j^\top(\|\mathbf{v}_j\| - \theta_j), \dot{\theta}_j = \frac{1}{\tau}(\|\mathbf{v}_j\|^2 - \theta_j)
· STDP trace: as in Definition 2.4

Barrier Potential. The Hamiltonian (Chapter 5) is augmented with

V_\text{barrier}(\mathbf{q}_j) = -\beta\log(1 - \|\mathbf{q}_j\|^2), \quad \beta > 0.

This diverges as \|\mathbf{q}_j\| \to 1, creating an unbounded repulsion from the boundary. Consequently, any trajectory starting with \|\mathbf{q}_j\| \le 1-\zeta_0 for some \zeta_0 > 0 remains in \{\|\mathbf{q}_j\| \le 1-\zeta\} for all time, where \zeta depends on \beta and the initial energy. This is the regime required by Theorem 4.1.

Homeostatic Scaling. For each capsule j, let E_j = \sum_i \|\mathbf{W}_{ij}\mathbf{u}_i\| be the total input norm. The scaling factor \alpha = T/(E_j + \varepsilon_0) ensures bounded activation. After scaling, if \|\mathbf{v}_j\| > 1 - \zeta, we clip weights and apply a penalty that repels the capsule from the boundary, consistent with the barrier potential.

4.4 Two-Timescale Analysis Under Markovian Noise

Let \mathbf{c}^{(k,t)} be the coupling after t routing iterations in plasticity block k, and \mathbf{W}^{(k)} the weights after k plasticity updates. Routing runs until the adaptive stopping criterion is met (inner loop); then a weight update occurs (outer loop).

Assumption 4.1 (Geometric Ergodicity of the Activation Process). The sequence of capsule activations \{\mathbf{u}^{(k)}\} forms a Markov chain that is geometrically ergodic with mixing time \tau_\text{mix} < \infty.

Lemma 4.2 (Spectral-Gap Bound on L_{c^*}). Let \lambda_{\min} > 0 be the smallest eigenvalue of the Hessian -\nabla^2_b\mathcal{L} at the fixed point \mathbf{c}^* (which is positive by Theorem 4.1's strict concavity). Then the map \mathbf{W} \mapsto \mathbf{c}^*(\mathbf{W}) is Lipschitz with constant

L_{c^*} \le \frac{\lambda_\text{max}^{(\text{cross})}}{\lambda_{\min}},

where \lambda_\text{max}^{(\text{cross})} is the maximum singular value of the cross-derivative \partial^2\mathcal{L}/(\partial\mathbf{c}\,\partial\mathbf{W}) evaluated at (\mathbf{c}^*, \mathbf{W}).

Proof. By the implicit function theorem applied to the first-order optimality condition \nabla_b\mathcal{L}(\mathbf{c}^*(\mathbf{W}), \mathbf{W}) = 0, the derivative D_\mathbf{W}\mathbf{c}^* = -(\nabla^2_b\mathcal{L})^{-1}\cdot(\partial^2\mathcal{L}/\partial b\,\partial\mathbf{W}). The operator norm is bounded by \lambda_{\min}^{-1}\cdot\lambda_\text{max}^{(\text{cross})}. Because the barrier ensures \lambda_{\min} \ge \lambda_0 > 0, L_{c^*} is finite and explicitly bounded. \square

Theorem 4.2 (Tracking Error Bound Under Markovian Noise). Let G be the Lipschitz constant of the plasticity update and B the number of forward passes between plasticity steps. Let \tau_\text{mix} be the mixing time of Assumption 4.1. Choose learning rate

\eta < \frac{\varepsilon_r(1-\rho_0)}{2B\,L_{c^*}\,G\,(1 + C_\pi\tau_\text{mix})},

where \rho_0 < 1 is the routing contraction factor from Theorem 4.1. Then for all sufficiently large blocks k, the routing error after adaptive stopping satisfies

\|\mathbf{c}^{(k,\text{final})} - \mathbf{c}^*(\mathbf{W}^{(k)})\| \le \varepsilon_r,

and the overall coupling tracks the optimal coefficients to within O(\eta(1 + C_\pi\tau_\text{mix})).

Proof. Within a block, routing contracts towards \mathbf{c}^*(\mathbf{W}^{(k)}) by factor \rho_0 < 1 (Theorem 4.1). The adaptive stopping rule ensures the final error is at most \varepsilon_r. Between blocks, the weight update changes \mathbf{c}^* by at most L_{c^*}\eta B G. The plasticity update \Delta\mathbf{W}^{(k)} is driven by the activation sequence \{\mathbf{u}^{(k)}\}, which is Markovian with mixing time \tau_\text{mix}. By the geometrically ergodic Markov chain central limit theorem (Meyn & Tweedie, Ch. 17), the effective noise in \Delta\mathbf{W}^{(k)} has variance bounded by the asymptotic variance of the ergodic average, which is at most G^2(1 + 2\sum_{n=1}^\infty C_\pi\rho_\pi^n) \le G^2(1 + 2C_\pi\tau_\text{mix}/(1-\rho_\pi)). The recurrence \varepsilon_{k+1} \le \rho_0^{T_k}\varepsilon_k + L_{c^*}\eta B G(1 + C_\pi\tau_\text{mix}), combined with \varepsilon_k \le \varepsilon_r, yields the bound under the stated condition on \eta. \square

4.5 Gene-Regulated Connectivity

Each capsule has a gene expression vector \mathbf{g}_c \in \mathbb{R}^G. A symmetric interaction matrix \mathbf{O} \in \mathbb{R}^{G\times G} governs connectivity.

Definition 4.2 (Connectivity Probability). The probability of a highway from capsule i to capsule j is

p_{ij} = \sigma(\mathbf{g}_i^\top\mathbf{O}\mathbf{g}_j),

where \sigma is the logistic sigmoid. Note p_{ij} \in (0,1) for any finite \mathbf{g}_i, \mathbf{g}_j, \mathbf{O}.

Proposition 4.1 (Differentiability). \frac{\partial p_{ij}}{\partial\mathbf{g}_i} = p_{ij}(1-p_{ij})\mathbf{O}\mathbf{g}_j and \frac{\partial p_{ij}}{\partial\mathbf{O}} = p_{ij}(1-p_{ij})\mathbf{g}_i\mathbf{g}_j^\top.

Update Rule for \mathbf{O}. Let B_{ij} \in \{0,1\} indicate whether a highway exists between capsules i and j. The update is

\Delta\mathbf{O} = \eta_O\sum_{(i,j)\in\text{observed}} (B_{ij} - p_{ij})\mathbf{g}_i\mathbf{g}_j^\top - \lambda_O\mathbf{O},

with \ell_2 regularisation. Symmetry is maintained via projection: \mathbf{O} \leftarrow \frac{1}{2}(\mathbf{O} + \mathbf{O}^\top).

Proposition 4.2 (Bounded Connectivity). Under the \ell_2 regularisation and symmetry constraint, \mathbf{O} remains in a compact set \{\|\mathbf{O}\|_F \le \sqrt{G/\lambda_O}\} almost surely, and all connectivity probabilities satisfy p_{ij} \in (\delta_p, 1-\delta_p) for some \delta_p > 0.

Proof. \Delta\mathbf{O} decreases \|\mathbf{O}\|_F whenever \|\mathbf{O}\|_F > \sqrt{G/\lambda_O}, because the cross-entropy gradient is bounded and the regularisation term dominates. Compactness follows. The sigmoid being bounded away from 0 and 1 on any compact domain for \mathbf{O} gives the \delta_p bound. \square

4.6 Distributed Nucleus and Generation Trigger

Each capsule monitors:

· \sigma_c^2: variance of \|\mathbf{u}_c\| over a time window
· \bar{\nu}_c: average update variance of incoming highways
· H_c: entropy of the discretised activation pattern

Definition 4.3 (Local Entropy H_c). Project the capsule output via PCA onto the leading r principal components, vector-quantise to n codebook vectors, compute empirical distribution \hat{p}_c(k) over a window of W steps:

H_c = -\sum_{k=1}^n \hat{p}_c(k)\log\hat{p}_c(k).

Hybrid Trigger with Capacity Brake.

\psi_c = \sigma\!\left((\sigma_c - \theta_\sigma) + (\theta_\nu - \bar{\nu}_c) + (H_c - \theta_H) - \lambda\frac{N_\text{caps}}{N_\max}\right),

where \sigma is the sigmoid, N_\text{caps} is the current capsule count, N_\max a hard capacity bound, and \lambda > 0.

Distributed Consensus. Each capsule periodically broadcasts (\psi_c, \mathbf{m}_c) where \mathbf{m}_c is its weight centroid. If a quorum of q capsules within a cluster have \psi_c > 0.5, a cluster meta-expansion (Section 3.5) is triggered. The gossip protocol converges in O(\log N) rounds on connected graphs.

Lemma 4.1 (Bounded Growth via Capacity Brake). For any input process and any initial capsule count N_0, the total number of capsule insertions is finite almost surely, and \lim_{t\to\infty} N_\text{caps}(t) \le N_\max.

Proof. Let I_t \in \{0,1\} indicate whether a capsule is inserted at time t. For any capsule c at time t, the insertion probability satisfies

P(\psi_c^{(t)} > 0.5) \le \sigma\!\left(-\lambda\frac{N_\text{caps}^{(t)}}{N_\max} + C_0\right) \le \sigma\!\left(-\lambda\frac{N_0 + \lfloor t/T_\text{quorum}\rfloor}{N_\max} + C_0\right),

where C_0 = |\theta_\sigma| + |\theta_\nu| + \log n bounds the non-brake terms, and T_\text{quorum} is the minimum time between consensus rounds. As N_\text{caps}^{(t)} \to N_\max, this probability decreases exponentially.

The total expected number of insertions satisfies

\mathbb{E}\!\left[\sum_{t=0}^\infty I_t\right] \le \sum_{t=0}^\infty N_\max\cdot\sigma\!\left(-\lambda\frac{N_0 + \lfloor t/T_\text{quorum}\rfloor}{N_\max} + C_0\right) < \infty,

since the sigmoid terms decay geometrically. By Markov's inequality, \sum_t I_t is finite almost surely. \square

4.7 Consolidation Against Catastrophic Forgetting

Maintain fast weights \mathbf{W}^\text{fast} and slow consolidated weights \mathbf{W}^\text{slow} per highway. Effective weight:

\mathbf{W}^\text{eff} = \alpha_c\mathbf{W}^\text{fast} + (1-\alpha_c)\mathbf{W}^\text{slow}, \quad \alpha_c = \sigma(\kappa(\theta_\text{cons} - \sigma_c)).

When output variance \sigma_c drops below \theta_\text{cons}, \alpha_c \to 0, freezing the representation. Slow weights follow an exponential moving average:

\mathbf{W}^\text{slow} \leftarrow \mathbf{W}^\text{slow} + \eta_\text{cons}(1-\alpha_c)(\mathbf{W}^\text{fast} - \mathbf{W}^\text{slow}).

Proposition 4.3 (Retention of Old Functions). Capsules that become highly selective for a stationary input component have \sigma_c \to 0, hence \alpha_c \to 0, and \mathbf{W}^\text{eff} \to \mathbf{W}^\text{slow}. Since \mathbf{W}^\text{slow} is updated with rate \eta_\text{cons}(1-\alpha_c) \to 0, the effective weights are frozen, preserving the learned mapping indefinitely.

4.8 Sparse Routing

Top-k sparse routing: retain only the k highways with highest agreement scores per source capsule; set all others to zero and renormalise.

Theorem 4.3 (Sparse Routing Error Bound). Assume the agreement scores a_{ij} = \mathbf{v}_j\cdot\hat{\mathbf{u}}_{j|i} are \sigma-subgaussian across j for each fixed i. Let \Delta = a_{(k)} - a_{(k+1)} be the score gap between the k-th and (k+1)-th ranked capsule. Then

\mathbb{E}[\|\mathbf{c}^\text{full} - \mathbf{c}^\text{sparse}\|_1] \le 2N\exp\!\left(-\frac{k\Delta^2}{2\sigma^2}\right),

where N is the number of target capsules.

Proof. The \ell_1 error is the total softmax mass assigned to excluded capsules. An excluded capsule j' with score a_{j'} \le a_{(k+1)} contributes to the error only if its score is elevated by noise. By subgaussian tail bound, P(a_{j'} > a_{(k)} - \Delta/2) \le \exp(-k\Delta^2/(2\sigma^2)) for each excluded capsule. Summing over the N-k \le N excluded capsules gives the stated bound. \square

