Chapter 5: Multi-Nuclei Hamiltonian System and Robust Stability

5.1 Port-Hamiltonian Formulation with Symmetric Positive Definite Damping

Let K nuclei govern a total of N capsules. Global state \mathbf{x} = [\mathbf{q}^\top, \mathbf{p}^\top]^\top \in \mathbb{R}^{2N}, capsule outputs \mathbf{q}, auxiliary momenta \mathbf{p}.

Hamiltonian:

H(\mathbf{q},\mathbf{p}) = \sum_{i=1}^N \frac{1}{2}\|\mathbf{p}_i\|^2 + \sum_{i=1}^N V_i(\mathbf{q}_i) + \sum_{(i,j)\in E}\Phi_{ij}(\mathbf{q}_i,\mathbf{q}_j),

where V_i(\mathbf{q}_i) = V_i^\text{act}(\mathbf{q}_i) - \beta\log(1-\|\mathbf{q}_i\|^2) contains the activation potential and barrier term, and \Phi_{ij}(\mathbf{q}_i,\mathbf{q}_j) = -\mathbf{q}_j\cdot(\mathbf{W}_{ij}\mathbf{q}_i).

The barrier term ensures that sublevel sets \{H \le c\} are compact.

Damping matrix. Let \mathbf{A} = \text{softmax}(\mathbf{X}\mathbf{X}^\top/\sqrt{d}) be the row-wise softmax attention matrix. Define:

\mathbf{J} = \mathbf{A} - \mathbf{A}^\top \quad \text{(skew-symmetric)},

\mathbf{R} = \kappa\mathbf{I} + \mathbf{A}\mathbf{A}^\top, \quad \kappa > 0.

Lemma 5.1 (Symmetric Positive Definiteness of \mathbf{R}). \mathbf{R} is symmetric and \mathbf{R} \succeq \kappa\mathbf{I} \succ 0 for all states \mathbf{x}.

Proof. \mathbf{A}\mathbf{A}^\top is a Gram matrix, hence symmetric positive semidefinite. Adding \kappa\mathbf{I} shifts all eigenvalues to at least \kappa > 0. \square

Dynamics:

\dot{\mathbf{x}} = (\mathbf{J}(\mathbf{x}) - \mathbf{R}(\mathbf{x}))\nabla H(\mathbf{x}).

Theorem 5.1 (Strict Passivity). Along any trajectory:

\dot{H} = -\nabla H^\top\mathbf{R}\nabla H \le -\kappa\|\nabla H\|^2 \le 0,

with equality only when \nabla H = 0.

Proof. \dot{H} = \nabla H^\top\dot{\mathbf{x}} = \nabla H^\top(\mathbf{J}-\mathbf{R})\nabla H = -\nabla H^\top\mathbf{R}\nabla H, since \nabla H^\top\mathbf{J}\nabla H = 0. By Lemma 5.1, \nabla H^\top\mathbf{R}\nabla H \ge \kappa\|\nabla H\|^2. \square

5.2 Asymptotic Stability for Fixed Topology

Theorem 5.2 (Asymptotic Stability for Fixed Topology). Let the topology (capsule and highway set) be fixed. Then every bounded trajectory converges to the equilibrium set \mathcal{E}^* := \{\mathbf{x} : \nabla H(\mathbf{x}) = 0\}.

Proof. H is non-increasing (Theorem 5.1) and bounded below on each compact sublevel set. By the classical LaSalle invariance principle applied to the continuous system with fixed state space, the trajectory approaches the largest invariant set within \{\dot{H}=0\} = \{\nabla H = 0\} = \mathcal{E}^*. \square

Remark 5.1 (Non-isolated Equilibria). We state convergence to the equilibrium set \mathcal{E}^* rather than to a single equilibrium. If the interaction terms have null spaces, \mathcal{E}^* may be a manifold. For generic choices of \mathbf{W}_{ij} (full Lebesgue measure in parameter space), Sard's theorem implies \mathcal{E}^* consists of isolated points, yielding point convergence. For non-generic configurations, convergence to a connected component of \mathcal{E}^* is still guaranteed.

5.3 Stability Under Structural Adaptation

Structural changes (capsule/highway insertions or prunings) occur at times t_1 < t_2 < \cdots At an event at time t_k, the state space dimension changes from 2N_{k-1} to 2N_k, and the Hamiltonian changes from H_{k-1} to H_k.

Proposition 5.1 (Bounded Energy Jumps). Each capsule insertion increases H by at most a constant \Delta H_\max (depending only on the initialisation range of new capsules, not on the current state). Each pruning decreases H by at most a constant.

Proof. For an insertion: the new kinetic term \frac{1}{2}\|\mathbf{p}_\text{new}\|^2 \le \frac{1}{2} by initialisation with bounded momenta; the barrier potential V_\text{barrier}(\mathbf{q}_\text{new}) is bounded above for any \|\mathbf{q}_\text{new}\| \le 1-\zeta_0; each new coupling \Phi_{ij} satisfies |\Phi_{ij}| \le \|\mathbf{W}_{ij}\|\|\mathbf{q}_i\|\|\mathbf{q}_j\| \le \|\mathbf{W}_{ij}\|, which is bounded by the gene-regulated initialisation (Proposition 4.2). Summing these bounds gives \Delta H_\max. \square

Definition 5.1 (Minimum Dwell-Time). Let \tau_d be the minimum time between consecutive structural events: \tau_d := \inf_{k\ge 1}(t_{k+1} - t_k) > 0. The minimum dwell-time is guaranteed by the gossip consensus protocol, which requires at least T_\text{quorum} steps between consecutive quorum decisions; hence \tau_d \ge T_\text{quorum} > 0.

Theorem 5.3 (Global Convergence under Structural Adaptation). Assume minimum dwell-time \tau_d \ge T_\text{quorum} > 0 and the capacity brake (Lemma 4.1). Then:

(i) The number of structural events is finite almost surely.
(ii) After the last event at time T^* < \infty (a.s.), the system has fixed topology.
(iii) Every trajectory converges to the equilibrium set \mathcal{E}^* of the final-topology Hamiltonian.

Proof.

Part (i). By Lemma 4.1, the total expected number of insertions is finite, hence the number of events is finite almost surely.

Part (ii). Finite events imply a last event time T^* = \max_k t_k < \infty almost surely.

Part (iii). We apply the hybrid LaSalle framework (Theorem 2.2). Between events t_k and t_{k+1}, the system runs the fixed-topology port-Hamiltonian dynamics, and H_k strictly decreases at rate \kappa\|\nabla H_k\|^2. By the minimum dwell-time \tau_d > 0, there is at least time \tau_d for dissipation between events. At each event, H changes by at most \Delta H_\max (Proposition 5.1). Since there are finitely many events (part (i)), the total energy injected is at most N_\max \cdot \Delta H_\max < \infty. After the last event T^*, the system has fixed topology and Theorem 5.2 applies, guaranteeing convergence to \mathcal{E}^*. \square

