Chapter 6: Continuous Causal Emergence 2.0 and Normalised Distributed Intelligence

6.1 Dimensionality Reduction for Tractable Estimation

The full state space of the capsule network has dimension d = 2N, making direct k-NN entropy estimation intractable for large N. We therefore restrict causal analysis to a projected low-dimensional state space.

Definition 6.1 (Projected State Space). Let d_\text{proj} \ll d be a fixed target dimension (e.g., d_\text{proj} = 10). Let \Pi: \mathbb{R}^{2N} \to \mathbb{R}^{d_\text{proj}} be the projection onto the leading d_\text{proj} principal components of the capsule output trajectory \{\mathbf{q}(t)\}. Define the projected state \mathbf{z}_t = \Pi(\mathbf{x}_t) \in \mathbb{R}^{d_\text{proj}}.

The causal power estimators of this chapter operate on the projected process \{\mathbf{z}_t\}. The causal structure of the projected process captures the dominant macro-scale dynamics.

6.2 Causal Primitives for Continuous-State Markov Chains

Let K(\mathbf{z}, d\mathbf{z}') be the Markov kernel of the projected process \{\mathbf{z}_t\}, with stationary density p(\mathbf{z}) on the compact domain \mathcal{Z} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^{d_\text{proj}}.

Definition 6.2 (Differential Conditional Entropy Rate).

h(Z^+|Z) = -\int_\mathcal{Z} p(\mathbf{z})\int_\mathcal{Z} K(\mathbf{z},\mathbf{z}')\log K(\mathbf{z},\mathbf{z}')\,d\mathbf{z}'\,d\mathbf{z}.

The reverse conditional entropy h(Z|Z^+) is defined via the time-reversed kernel \tilde{K}(\mathbf{z}',\mathbf{z}) = K(\mathbf{z},\mathbf{z}')p(\mathbf{z})/p(\mathbf{z}').

Let h_\max = \log\,\text{vol}(\mathcal{Z}) be the maximum differential entropy (uniform distribution on the compact \mathcal{Z}). Note that under the compact support and non-degenerate densities, h(Z^+|Z) \le h(Z^+) \le h_\max, so the following definitions yield quantities bounded by 1.

Definition 6.3 (Continuous Determinism, Specificity, Causal Power).

\text{det}_c = 1 - \frac{h(Z^+|Z)}{h_\max}, \quad \text{spec}_c = 1 - \frac{h(Z|Z^+)}{h_\max}, \quad \text{CP}_c = \text{det}_c + \text{spec}_c - 1.

All quantities lie in [0,1] under the normalisation.

Estimation. Given trajectory \{\mathbf{z}_t\}_{t=1}^N, estimate h(Z^+, Z) and h(Z) using the k-NN Kozachenko-Leonenko estimator on the joint and marginal processes in \mathbb{R}^{d_\text{proj}}, then compute \hat{h}(Z^+|Z) = \hat{h}(Z^+, Z) - \hat{h}(Z).

Theorem 6.1 (Consistency of \widehat{\text{CP}}_c in Projected Space). Assume: (i) the projected process \{\mathbf{z}_t\} is stationary and geometrically ergodic with mixing time \tau_\text{mix}^\Pi; (ii) the conditional density p(\mathbf{z}_{t+1}|\mathbf{z}_t) is Lipschitz and bounded away from zero and infinity on the compact support \mathcal{Z}. Then

\widehat{\text{CP}}_c \xrightarrow{\mathbb{P}} \text{CP}_c \quad \text{as } N\to\infty,

with rate O\!\left(\left(\frac{N}{\tau_\text{mix}^\Pi}\right)^{-1/(d_\text{proj}+2)}\right).

Proof. The k-NN estimators of differential entropy are consistent under conditions (i) and (ii) for the d_\text{proj}-dimensional projected process. The effective sample size is N/\tau_\text{mix}^\Pi due to the mixing time correction. The rate follows from the standard k-NN estimator rate O(n^{-1/(d+2)}) with effective n = N/\tau_\text{mix}^\Pi and d = d_\text{proj}. \square

6.3 Hierarchical Apportionment with Downward Causation

Let \phi: \mathcal{Z} \to \mathcal{S} be a coarse-graining. The macro-kernel is

K^\phi(s, s') = \frac{\int_{\phi^{-1}(s)} p(\mathbf{z})\int_{\phi^{-1}(s')} K(\mathbf{z},d\mathbf{z}')\,d\mathbf{z}}{\int_{\phi^{-1}(s)} p(\mathbf{z})\,d\mathbf{z}}.

Compute \text{CP}_\text{micro} = \text{CP}_c and \text{CP}_\text{macro} = \text{CP}(K^\phi).

Lemma 6.1 (Non-Negativity of Apportionment Weights). Define U = \max(0, \text{CP}_\text{macro} - \text{CP}_\text{micro}) \ge 0 and D = \max(0, \text{CP}_\text{micro} - \text{CP}_\text{macro}) \ge 0. Then U, D \ge 0 by construction. Furthermore, at any given scale transition, exactly one of U or D is positive (or both are zero when \text{CP}_\text{macro} = \text{CP}_\text{micro}). The normalised weights w^{up}_k = U_k / \sum_\ell (U_\ell + D_\ell) and w^{down}_k = D_k / \sum_\ell (U_\ell + D_\ell) are non-negative and sum to one:

\sum_k (w_k^\text{up} + w_k^\text{down}) = 1.

Proof. Non-negativity is immediate from the max with zero. The normalisation is by definition. \square

Definition 6.4 (Emergent Complexity \mathcal{E}). With weights from Lemma 6.1:

\mathcal{E} = -\sum_k\left(w_k^\text{up}\log w_k^\text{up} + w_k^\text{down}\log w_k^\text{down}\right), \quad (0\log 0 = 0).

Since there are m scale transitions, contributing 2m weights summing to one, \mathcal{E} \le \log(2m).

6.4 Normalised Distributed Intelligence

For K nuclei, let N_k(t) be the discretised state of nucleus k (via the same PCA + vector-quantisation pipeline as Definition 4.3). The multi-information is

I(N_1;\dots;N_K) = \sum_{k=1}^K H(N_k) - H(N_1,\dots,N_K) \ge 0.

Definition 6.5 (Normalised Distributed Intelligence).

\tilde{\mathcal{D}} = \frac{\bar{\mathcal{E}}}{\log(2m)} + \frac{I}{\sum_{k=1}^K H(N_k)},

where \bar{\mathcal{E}} is the path-averaged emergent complexity over many coarse-graining paths.

Proposition 6.1 (\tilde{\mathcal{D}} \in [0,2]). The first term \bar{\mathcal{E}}/\log(2m) \in [0,1] by Lemma 6.1 and the entropy upper bound. The second term I/\sum_k H(N_k) \in [0,1] since I \le \sum_k H(N_k). Therefore \tilde{\mathcal{D}} \in [0,2].

Proof. First term: 0 \le \bar{\mathcal{E}} \le \log(2m) by the Shannon entropy bound for a distribution over 2m atoms. Second term: H(N_1,\dots,N_K) \ge 0 implies I \le \sum_k H(N_k); also I \ge 0 by non-negativity of KL divergence. \square

