Appendix A: Mathematical Background

This appendix reviews the essential concepts from measure theory, dynamical systems, and information theory that underpin the thesis. All definitions are provided to make the document self-contained.

A.1 Probability Spaces and \sigma-Algebras

Definition A.1 (\sigma-algebra). A collection \mathcal{F} of subsets of a set W is a \sigma-algebra if: (i) W \in \mathcal{F}; (ii) A \in \mathcal{F} \implies W \setminus A \in \mathcal{F}; (iii) A_1, A_2, \dots \in \mathcal{F} \implies \bigcup_{n=1}^\infty A_n \in \mathcal{F}. The pair (W, \mathcal{F}) is a measurable space. A probability measure \mu on (W, \mathcal{F}) is a function \mu: \mathcal{F} \to [0,1] with \mu(\emptyset)=0, \mu(W)=1, and countable additivity.

Definition A.2 (Pushforward measure). Given a measurable mapping f: (W, \mathcal{F}, \mu) \to (X, \mathcal{G}), the pushforward f_*\mu is the measure on (X, \mathcal{G}) defined by (f_*\mu)(B) = \mu(f^{-1}(B)).

Definition A.3 (Absolute continuity). A measure \nu is absolutely continuous with respect to \mu (\nu \ll \mu) if \mu(A)=0 \implies \nu(A)=0. By the Radon–Nikodym theorem, \nu \ll \mu iff there exists a density f = d\nu/d\mu \in L^1(\mu).

Definition A.4 (Sub-\sigma-algebra). A \sigma-algebra \mathcal{G} \subseteq \mathcal{F} is a sub-\sigma-algebra. A function h: W \to \mathbb{R} is \mathcal{G}-measurable if for every Borel set B, h^{-1}(B) \in \mathcal{G}.

Lemma A.1 (Atoms of \sigma(\mathcal{H})). Let \mathcal{H} \in \mathcal{F} and define \sigma(\mathcal{H}) = \{\emptyset, \mathcal{H}, W \setminus \mathcal{H}, W\}. Its atoms are \mathcal{H} and each singleton \{w\} for w \in W \setminus \mathcal{H} (up to indistinguishability). Any \sigma(\mathcal{H})-measurable function is constant on each atom.

A.2 Markov Chains and Kernels

Definition A.5 (Markov kernel). A kernel K: (W, \mathcal{F}) \times \mathcal{F} \to [0,1] is a function such that for each w, K(w, \cdot) is a probability measure, and for each A \in \mathcal{F}, K(\cdot, A) is measurable.

A time-homogeneous Markov chain evolves as \mathbb{P}(X_{t+1} \in A \mid X_t = w) = K(w, A). The n-step kernel is K^n.

Definition A.6 (Geometric ergodicity). A Markov chain with stationary distribution \pi is geometrically ergodic if there exist \rho \in (0,1) and C < \infty such that

\|K^n(w, \cdot) - \pi\|_{TV} \le C \rho^n \quad \text{for all } w,

where \|\cdot\|_{TV} is total variation distance. The mixing time is \tau_{\text{mix}}(\epsilon) = \min\{n: \sup_w \|K^n(w,\cdot)-\pi\|_{TV} \le \epsilon\}; we often use \tau_{\text{mix}} = \tau_{\text{mix}}(1/4).

A.3 Port-Hamiltonian Dynamics and Stability

Definition A.7 (Port-Hamiltonian system). A dynamical system

\dot{x} = [J(x) - R(x)] \nabla H(x) + g(x)u, \quad y = g(x)^\top \nabla H(x),

with J(x)^\top = -J(x), R(x) \succeq 0, is passive because \dot H \le u^\top y.

Theorem A.1 (LaSalle invariance principle). For an autonomous system \dot{x} = f(x) with a Lyapunov function V \ge 0 such that \dot V \le 0 on a compact positively invariant set \Omega, every trajectory converges to the largest invariant subset of \{x \in \Omega : \dot V(x)=0\}.

Theorem A.2 (Hybrid LaSalle, simplified). Consider a hybrid system with flow map F and jump map G on \mathbb{R}^n. Suppose there is a Lyapunov function V such that \dot V \le -\alpha V during flows, V(G(x)) \le V(x) + \Delta V_{\max} at jumps, and the number of jumps is finite. Then all bounded solutions converge to the largest invariant set of \{V = \text{const}\}.

A.4 Two-Timescale Stochastic Approximation

Consider the iteration

x_{k+1} = x_k + \eta_k [h(x_k) + \xi_k],

where h is Lipschitz and \xi_k is a zero-mean noise process. Under diminishing \eta_k, the ODE limit is \dot{x} = h(x). For constant step size \eta, the iterates remain within an O(\eta) neighbourhood of the equilibrium set of h, assuming the noise is sufficiently mixing (Kushner & Yin 2003).

When h implicitly depends on a fast transient process, a two-timescale analysis is used. The slow variable tracks the quasi-equilibrium of the fast process; the error depends on the Lipschitz constant of the map from slow to fast equilibrium and the noise scaling.

A.5 Differential Entropy and k-NN Estimator

For a continuous random variable Z with density p, the differential entropy is

h(Z) = -\int p(z) \log p(z) \, dz.

Given i.i.d. samples \{z_i\}_{i=1}^N, the k-NN estimator (Kozachenko & Leonenko 1987) is

\hat{h}(Z) = \log \frac{C_d(k)}{N} + \frac{d}{N} \sum_{i=1}^N \log \epsilon_i,

where \epsilon_i is the distance to the k-th nearest neighbour, C_d(k) depends only on d and k. Under Lipschitz densities and geometric ergodicity, it is consistent with rate O(N^{-1/(d+2)}).

