Appendix B: Detailed Proofs

B.1 Proof of Theorem 3.2 (Algorithmic Undetectability)

Recall: Internal algorithm M has access to s_0, T|_{\mathcal{H}}, and samples absolutely continuous w.r.t. \mu|_{\mathcal{H}}. Output \hat{\chi} = M(\dots). We prove that \mu(\{w \in \mathcal{B} : \hat{\chi}(w)=1\}) = 0.

Proof details:

1. Information structure: The complete information accessible to M can be represented by the \sigma-algebra generated by the random variables it observes. Since all observations are draws from measures supported on \mathcal{H}, the measurable functions produced by M are \sigma(\mathcal{H})-measurable. Indeed, every query to T(h,\cdot) returns a value in \mathcal{H}; finite sampling from \mu|_{\mathcal{H}} yields points in \mathcal{H}. Therefore any indicator function f = \mathbf{1}_S constructed by M must be such that S \in \sigma(\mathcal{H}).
2. Structure of \sigma(\mathcal{H}): For a fixed set \mathcal{H}, the \sigma-algebra \sigma(\mathcal{H}) = \{\emptyset, \mathcal{H}, W\setminus\mathcal{H}, W\}. However, because the agent may also have access to additional randomisation independent of the world (e.g., internal coin flips), the actual measurable sets are unions of atoms that are essentially \mathcal{H} and the points in W \setminus \mathcal{H}. The key point is that points inside W \setminus \mathcal{H} are indistinguishable: any \sigma(\mathcal{H})-measurable function must take the same value on all points in W \setminus \mathcal{H}. This can be shown by considering the conditional expectation with respect to \sigma(\mathcal{H}). For any \sigma(\mathcal{H})-measurable g, g(w) = \mathbb{E}[g \mid \sigma(\mathcal{H})](w), which is constant on atoms. The atoms of \sigma(\mathcal{H}) are \mathcal{H} and the singletons of W \setminus \mathcal{H} only if the agent has information that separates them, but since all observations originate from \mathcal{H}, the agent cannot distinguish points in W \setminus \mathcal{H}. Thus, for any \sigma(\mathcal{H})-measurable set A with positive measure, either A \cap \mathcal{H} has positive measure, or A contains all of W \setminus \mathcal{H} (up to null sets). More precisely, any \sigma(\mathcal{H})-measurable set A can be written as A = (A \cap \mathcal{H}) \cup (A \cap (W \setminus \mathcal{H})), but A \cap (W \setminus \mathcal{H}) must either be \emptyset or all of W \setminus \mathcal{H} (modulo null sets). Proof: Suppose there exist w_1, w_2 \in W \setminus \mathcal{H} with w_1 \in A, w_2 \notin A. Then the indicator of A would separate w_1 and w_2, contradicting that it is \sigma(\mathcal{H})-measurable (since all \sigma(\mathcal{H})-measurable functions are constant on W \setminus \mathcal{H}). Therefore A \cap (W \setminus \mathcal{H}) is either empty or the whole set W \setminus \mathcal{H}. Consequently, any \sigma(\mathcal{H})-measurable subset of W \setminus \mathcal{H} with positive measure must equal W \setminus \mathcal{H} up to a null set.
3. Application to \mathcal{B}: The blind spot set \mathcal{B} \subseteq W \setminus \mathcal{H} and \mu(\mathcal{B}) > 0. If \hat{\chi} were to correctly identify \mathcal{B} on a set S \subseteq \mathcal{B} with \mu(S) > 0, then \hat{\chi}^{-1}(1) \supseteq S is a \sigma(\mathcal{H})-measurable set containing a positive-measure subset of W \setminus \mathcal{H}. By the above property, this forces \hat{\chi}^{-1}(1) \cap (W \setminus \mathcal{H}) = W \setminus \mathcal{H} up to null sets. Hence \hat{\chi} must output 1 on almost all of W \setminus \mathcal{H}, including points outside \mathcal{B} (since \mathcal{B} \subsetneq W \setminus \mathcal{H} typically). Thus \hat{\chi} cannot have high accuracy specifically on \mathcal{B}. The probability of correct identification over \mathcal{B} is at most the probability that the randomisation causes \hat{\chi}=1 on a subset of \mathcal{B} of full measure; but this cannot happen because any such success set would have to be all of W \setminus \mathcal{H}, contradicting the fact that points in W \setminus (\mathcal{H} \cup \mathcal{B}) are wrongly labelled. Hence \mu(\{w \in \mathcal{B}: \hat{\chi}(w)=1\}) = 0. \square

B.2 Proof of Theorem 4.1 (Routing Uniqueness and Convergence)

Detailed argument: The routing objective is \mathcal{L}(b) = \sum_j \tilde{f}(\mathbf{q}_j(\mathbf{c}(b))) \cdot \mathbf{q}_j(\mathbf{c}(b)), where \mathbf{c}(b)_{ij} = \frac{e^{b_{ij}}}{\sum_k e^{b_{ik}}}. Compute Hessian of \mathcal{L} w.r.t. b_{ij}. By the barrier, \|\mathbf{q}_j\| \le 1-\zeta, so \tilde{f}'(\|\mathbf{q}_j\|) = \text{sech}^2(\|\mathbf{q}_j\|) \ge \text{sech}^2(1-\zeta) > 0. In this regime, \tilde{f} is strictly increasing and its Jacobian is positive definite. The mapping b \mapsto \mathbf{c} via softmax is smooth, and its Jacobian has a specific Kronecker structure. The Hessian \nabla^2_b \mathcal{L} is a block matrix whose diagonal blocks are negative definite, and the off-diagonals are bounded. The Gershgorin circle theorem ensures that for sufficiently large \mu_{\min} from the barrier, the entire Hessian is negative definite uniformly. Hence \mathcal{L} is strictly concave on the convex set of logits (unconstrained). The routing iteration is coordinate ascent on the logits: each step increases \mathcal{L}. Because the objective is strictly concave and the feasible set (product of probability simplices) is compact, there is a unique global maximum. The iterative scheme converges to it at a linear rate near the optimum. \square

B.3 Proof of Lemma 4.1 (Capacity Brake — Finite Insertions)

Refined bound: For insertion indicator I_t, we have P(I_t=1) \le N_{\max} \cdot \sigma(-\lambda N_{\text{caps}}^{(t)}/N_{\max} + C_0). Since N_{\text{caps}}^{(t)} \ge N_0 + \lfloor t/T_{\text{quorum}}\rfloor, the term \sigma(\dots) decays geometrically with t. The sum \sum_t P(I_t=1) < \infty. Then \mathbb{E}[\sum_t I_t] = \sum_t \mathbb{E}[I_t] \le \sum_t P(I_t=1) < \infty. By Markov's inequality, for any \epsilon>0, P(\sum_t I_t > M) \le \mathbb{E}[\sum_t I_t]/M \to 0 as M\to\infty. Thus \sum_t I_t < \infty almost surely, i.e., only finitely many insertions. No independence is assumed. \square

